    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "I think from their party perspective, I think they need to take a real self look because, you know, Israel is such a dear friend of the United States, and Andy's committee behavior or anti-Jewish behavior should never be tolerated at all. We've always, you know, here's the best hope of democracy in the Middle East and stability force in the Middle East. My concern is, or the thought of being that they continue down this path is, you know, Jewish folks who believe who are part of the Democratic Party, they have to really sit back and answer the question, does my party still reflect or regulate? And I think that's going to be something that is going to be coming up in election five, coming up in the base that we have. It was very disheartening for me, not only the issue that you spoke of with the on the board, but also we had a bill that we've been working on for many years with Congressman Roscomb and Franklin Adler and DeWitch and others from the Democratic side on adding language into anti-Semitic language that could identify on college campuses and education. And we tried to reenter that bill last year. Never could get anybody on the Democratic side who had been co-sponsors of this bill. In two to three previous Congresses, they would not sign on the bill signing a language problem with the bill. The reality is they just didn't want the leadership didn't want them on the bill. It would antagonize, I guess, the folks in their caucus that are turned anti-Semitic, so they wouldn't get on it. So the president himself actually had to do an executive order. At the end of last year, I was privileged to be there when he signed it that dealt with the very essence of our bill in making sure that identification of anti-Semitic speech on the college campus can be identified.",
